If the pressure is constant, then dp/dt = 0, and 

(15)
produces the definition of the heat capacity at constant
pressure:q_t = c_p \frac{\partial T}{\partial t}\ \text{at constant pressure}

where:c_p = \frac{\partial h}{\partial T}

Which is Thompkins eq. 1.27.

If you don’t like the hand-waving limits I took to get c_v in terms of u
in Section 

Section 2, equation 

(7) above, you can redo it in the
same way as in 

(15) to get:q_t = \frac{\partial u}{\partial T} \frac{dT}{dt} + \left( \frac{\partial u}{\partial v} + p \right) \frac{dv}{dt}

and taking constant volume (dv/dt = 0):\begin{aligned}
c_v &= \frac{\partial u}{\partial T} \\
q_t &= c_v \frac{\partial T}{\partial t}\ \text{at constant volume}
\end{aligned}

This derivation is a little longer than the one in Thompkins, but it doesn’t rely on any
confusing combinations state variables and processes like this one in Thompkins eq. 1.17:c_p = \left( \frac{\partial q}{\partial T} \right)_P

which tend to make mathematicians queasy, given what we’ve already said about \dbar q not being a perfect differential.
